-- Q4561: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Required in all group audit engagements regardless of circumstances","Not permitted under any auditing standards or frameworks","Permitted for non-issuer audits when conditions are met, dividing responsibility","Required only when the component auditor is a foreign subsidiary"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): For non-issuer audits, the group engagement partner may make reference to a component auditor in the group audit report, which divides responsibility between the group and component auditors. AU-C 600 permits this when the component auditor is independent and complies with relevant standards.
Wrong (A): Reference to a component auditor is permitted, not required. The group auditor may choose not to make reference.
Wrong (B): Reference is permitted for non-issuer audits under AU-C 600, though it is not permitted under PCAOB standards for issuer audits.
Wrong (D): The decision to make reference is not limited to foreign subsidiaries; it applies to any component auditor meeting the conditions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4561;

-- Q4562: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unmodified opinion without obtaining additional evidence","Require the user entity to terminate the service organization relationship","Disclaim an opinion without first attempting to obtain sufficient evidence","Obtain a SOC report, perform tests directly, or use another auditor for procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 402, when the user auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence from the user entity alone, options include obtaining a SOC 1 report, contacting the service organization to request specific procedures, or engaging another auditor to perform procedures at the service organization.
Wrong (A): Issuing an unmodified opinion without sufficient evidence violates AU-C 402 and general auditing standards requiring adequate evidence.
Wrong (B): The auditor has no authority to require the user entity to terminate a business relationship with a service organization.
Wrong (C): The auditor must first exhaust available options for obtaining evidence before considering a scope limitation or disclaimer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4562;

-- Q4563: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The specialist''s fee arrangement and billing terms with the audit firm","Whether the specialist uses the same valuation software as the audit firm","The specialist''s political affiliations and personal voting history","Whether the specialist has relationships with the entity affecting objectivity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 620, the auditor must evaluate whether the specialist has financial interests, business relationships, or employment ties with the entity that could compromise objectivity. This is especially important when the specialist was engaged by management.
Wrong (A): Fee arrangements with the audit firm relate to the firm''s own independence, not the specialist''s objectivity regarding the entity.
Wrong (B): Software compatibility is an operational matter, not a factor in evaluating the specialist''s objectivity.
Wrong (C): Political affiliations are not relevant to evaluating professional objectivity under auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4563;

-- Q4567: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recalculation of account balances and transaction totals","Observation of duties performed and inspection of authorization signatures","Sending confirmation letters to customers and vendors","Performing analytical procedures on disbursement account balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Observation and inspection are the most effective procedures for testing segregation of duties under AU-C 330. The auditor observes that different individuals perform incompatible functions and inspects authorization signatures and access logs to verify the control operates as designed.
Wrong (A): Recalculation tests the mathematical accuracy of amounts, not whether duties are properly segregated among personnel.
Wrong (C): Confirmations verify account balances with third parties and do not address internal segregation of duties.
Wrong (D): Analytical procedures identify unusual trends or relationships but cannot confirm that specific individuals are performing only authorized functions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4567;

-- Q4568: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only one occurrence because the control is considered well-designed","A sufficient number of occurrences, typically each or most of them","Only the single most recent occurrence of the reconciliation","Only the occurrences performed during the year-end close period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, for controls operating infrequently such as monthly reconciliations (12 times per year), the auditor typically tests each occurrence or most of them. The small population does not lend itself to sampling, so testing all 12 provides the strongest evidence of consistent operating effectiveness.
Wrong (A): Testing only one occurrence is insufficient for a manual control; a single instance cannot demonstrate consistency throughout the period.
Wrong (C): Testing only the most recent occurrence provides no evidence about whether the control operated effectively during earlier months.
Wrong (D): Limiting testing to year-end ignores the remaining months and does not demonstrate effectiveness throughout the audit period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4568;

-- Q4569: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Tests two different internal controls at the same time","Tests control effectiveness and provides substantive evidence simultaneously","Tests the same control procedures at two separate locations","Tests the identical control at two different points in time"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A dual-purpose test simultaneously serves as both a test of controls and a substantive test of details under AU-C 330. For example, checking a purchase transaction for proper authorization (control) and correct dollar amount (substantive) in a single procedure.
Wrong (A): Testing two different controls simultaneously is not a dual-purpose test; the dual nature refers to combining control and substantive objectives.
Wrong (C): Testing a control at two locations is extended testing of the same control, not a dual-purpose test.
Wrong (D): Testing the same control at two time points is testing over a period, not a dual-purpose test.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4569;

-- Q4572: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor observing management as they perform the control procedure","The auditor independently executing the control to verify expected results","Management repeating the control procedure at the auditor''s specific request","The auditor recalculating financial statement balances for mathematical accuracy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Reperformance involves the auditor independently executing the same procedure or control that entity personnel performed, to determine whether the control produces correct results. Under AU-C 330, independently reconciling a bank statement verifies that management''s reconciliation control operates effectively.
Wrong (A): Observing management perform the control is observation, a separate audit procedure from reperformance.
Wrong (C): When management repeats a control, the auditor is not independently executing it, which is the defining characteristic of reperformance.
Wrong (D): Recalculating financial statement balances is a substantive procedure, not a test of controls through reperformance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4572;

-- Q4573: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Test once or a few times, combined with testing supporting IT general controls","Test every single transaction that the automated system has processed","Test the control the same number of times as any comparable manual control","Skip testing automated controls because they are considered inherently reliable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Automated controls are inherently consistent — once programmed correctly, they perform identically every time. Under AU-C 330, the auditor typically tests the control once or a small number of times, combined with testing IT general controls (program change controls, access controls) that ensure the automated control has not been altered.
Wrong (B): Testing every transaction is unnecessary because automated controls produce identical results each time they execute.
Wrong (C): Automated controls do not require the same sample sizes as manual controls due to their inherent consistency.
Wrong (D): Automated controls still require testing; the auditor must verify they are programmed correctly and ITGCs are effective.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4573;

-- Q4574: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assess the impact on risk and determine what additional procedures are needed","Ignore the control failure if it occurred during an earlier interim period","Immediately issue an adverse opinion on the financial statements","Continue to rely on the control for the entire audit period regardless"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, when a control failure is discovered, the auditor must assess its impact on the risk assessment and planned audit procedures. The auditor determines the nature and extent of additional substantive procedures needed for the affected period and considers whether the failure indicates a systemic problem.
Wrong (B): A control failure during any part of the period is relevant and cannot be ignored simply because it occurred early in the period.
Wrong (C): A control failure does not automatically require an adverse opinion; the auditor must first evaluate the impact and perform additional procedures.
Wrong (D): Continuing to rely on a control known to be ineffective during part of the period violates auditing standards requiring evidence of operating effectiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4574;

-- Q4576: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Support a lower assessed control risk, allowing reduced substantive procedures","Ensure that the audited entity remains profitable during the period","Prepare the entity''s financial statements on management''s behalf","Determine the appropriate interest rate for the entity''s outstanding debt"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, the primary reason for testing controls is to support a lower assessed level of control risk, which allows the auditor to reduce the nature, timing, or extent of substantive procedures. If controls are effective, less substantive testing may be needed.
Wrong (B): Evaluating the entity''s profitability is not an objective of tests of controls; controls relate to financial reporting reliability.
Wrong (C): The auditor does not prepare the entity''s financial statements; that is management''s responsibility under AU-C 200.
Wrong (D): Determining interest rates is a financial management decision unrelated to the purpose of testing internal controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4576;

-- Q4577: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Test all 250 occurrences without exception during the audit period","Test only one occurrence since the control operates on a daily basis","Select a sample sufficient to provide a reasonable basis for conclusions","Test only the occurrences processed during the final month of December"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, for controls that operate frequently such as daily, the auditor uses sampling to select a sufficient number of occurrences for testing. Sample size depends on the desired level of assurance, the expected deviation rate, and the planned reliance on the control.
Wrong (A): Testing all 250 occurrences is impractical and unnecessary; sampling provides sufficient evidence for frequently operating controls.
Wrong (B): Testing only one occurrence does not provide sufficient evidence of operating effectiveness throughout the period for a manual daily control.
Wrong (D): Limiting testing to December ignores the remaining eleven months and does not demonstrate year-round operating effectiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4577;

-- Q4578: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They directly process and record the entity''s financial transactions","They are specifically required by federal or state tax regulations","They ensure automated application controls function effectively and data is reliable","They determine the overall profitability and financial health of the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): IT general controls — including access controls, program change controls, and computer operations — support the reliable functioning of automated application controls and data integrity. Under AU-C 315, without effective ITGCs, automated controls may not be trustworthy because unauthorized changes could compromise their effectiveness.
Wrong (A): ITGCs do not directly process transactions; application controls handle transaction processing while ITGCs support the environment.
Wrong (B): ITGCs are required by auditing standards for audit reliance purposes, not by tax regulations.
Wrong (D): ITGCs relate to the reliability of IT systems and controls, not to measuring or determining the entity''s profitability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4578;

-- Q4579: Tests of Controls [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately issue a qualified opinion based on the single deviation found","Ignore the deviation since only one instance was identified in the sample","Evaluate whether the deviation is isolated or systemic and reassess control risk","Expand the sample to cover the entire population of disbursement transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, a deviation in a test of controls requires the auditor to evaluate whether it represents an isolated occurrence or a systemic weakness. The auditor considers the nature and cause of the deviation, whether the deviation rate exceeds the tolerable rate, and the impact on assessed control risk.
Wrong (A): A single control deviation does not automatically require a qualified opinion; the auditor must first evaluate its significance and cause.
Wrong (B): Ignoring a deviation violates auditing standards, which require the auditor to evaluate every identified deviation regardless of frequency.
Wrong (D): Expanding to 100% testing is rarely practical or necessary; the auditor should evaluate the deviation and adjust the audit approach accordingly.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4579;

-- Q4581: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Must formally justify this decision to the entity''s audit committee","Must still test controls over all significant risk areas regardless","Cannot issue an unmodified opinion under a fully substantive approach","Assesses control risk at maximum and designs substantive procedures accordingly"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, when the auditor elects not to test controls, control risk is assessed at the maximum level. The auditor then designs substantive procedures with sufficient nature, timing, and extent to address the higher assessed risks at the assertion level. This is common for smaller entities or where control testing would not be efficient.
Wrong (A): While the auditor documents the approach, there is no specific requirement to justify a substantive approach to the audit committee.
Wrong (B): A substantive approach means the auditor relies entirely on substantive procedures rather than testing controls, even for significant risks.
Wrong (C): An unmodified opinion can still be issued if sufficient substantive evidence is obtained to support the financial statement assertions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4581;

-- Q4582: Tests of Controls [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Has been consistently applied by personnel throughout the entire period","Generates sufficient supporting documentation for the audit work file","Requires additional personnel or resources to operate as intended","Is suitably designed to prevent or detect material misstatements at assertions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315, evaluating design effectiveness means assessing whether the control, if operating as designed, would be effective in preventing or detecting and correcting material misstatements at the relevant assertion level. Design effectiveness is evaluated separately from operating effectiveness.
Wrong (A): Consistent application throughout the period relates to operating effectiveness, not design effectiveness. Design is assessed independently of actual operation.
Wrong (B): Documentation sufficiency is an administrative consideration, not the focus of evaluating whether a control''s design addresses relevant assertion-level risks.
Wrong (C): Staffing requirements are operational resource matters, not criteria for evaluating whether the control''s design effectively addresses misstatement risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4582;

-- Q4583: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["At least once every five years under a long-term rotation cycle","At least every other year under a biennial testing rotation","In each audit period, though extent may be reduced through rotation","Only when the entity specifically requests control testing be performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, if the auditor plans to rely on controls, some controls must be tested in each audit period. The auditor may use a rotational approach where different controls are tested each year, but a control should not go untested for more than two consecutive periods.
Wrong (A): A five-year cycle is too long; auditing standards require testing within a maximum three-year rotation for any individual control.
Wrong (B): Every other year exceeds the standard''s expectation that some controls be tested each period, with no control untested beyond two consecutive years.
Wrong (D): The decision to test controls is based on the auditor''s planned reliance, not on requests from the entity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4583;

-- Q4584: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Financial statements for errors in presentation and disclosure","Customer correspondence for indications of complaints or disputes","Bank statements for unusual deposit patterns or irregularities","Documents for evidence the control was performed, such as approvals or signatures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Inspection of documents as a test of controls involves examining evidence that the control was performed, such as approval signatures on purchase orders, initials on reconciliations, and exception reports. Under AU-C 330, this evidence demonstrates that the control activity was carried out as designed.
Wrong (A): Inspecting financial statements for presentation errors is a substantive procedure, not a test of the operating effectiveness of a control.
Wrong (B): Reviewing customer correspondence relates to substantive procedures for receivables or revenue, not testing purchase approval controls.
Wrong (C): Examining bank statements for unusual deposits is a substantive procedure unrelated to testing the purchase approval control.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4584;

-- Q4585: Tests of Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No additional testing is needed since IT controls remain inherently consistent","The auditor should defer all control testing until the following audit period","The auditor should test only the controls implemented after the system change","The auditor may need to test controls both before and after the change"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, when significant IT system changes occur during the audit period, the auditor may need to test controls in place before the change for the pre-change period and controls implemented after the change for the remaining period. This ensures effective controls existed throughout the entire period of reliance.
Wrong (A): IT system changes can alter how controls operate, so the auditor cannot assume consistency across the change without additional testing.
Wrong (B): Deferring all testing to the next period fails to obtain evidence about control effectiveness during the current audit period.
Wrong (C): Testing only post-change controls ignores the portion of the period before the change, leaving a gap in the auditor''s evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4585;

-- Q4586: Analytical Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["During both the planning and overall review phases of the audit","Only during the substantive testing phase of the engagement","Only during the final overall review phase of the audit","Only during the initial planning phase of the engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 520, analytical procedures are required during two phases: (1) the planning phase, to identify areas of potential misstatement and assist in risk assessment, and (2) the overall review phase near the end of the audit, to assess whether the financial statements are consistent with the auditor''s understanding.
Wrong (B): Substantive analytical procedures are optional, not required. The mandatory phases are planning and overall review.
Wrong (C): Limiting required analytics to only the final review omits the planning phase requirement established by AU-C 520.
Wrong (D): Limiting required analytics to only planning omits the overall review requirement established by AU-C 520.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4586;

-- Q4587: Analytical Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Provide substantive audit evidence about specific account balances","Identify unusual relationships indicating areas of increased misstatement risk","Confirm the mathematical accuracy of the prior year''s financial statements","Test the operating effectiveness of the entity''s internal controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, planning analytical procedures help the auditor identify unusual or unexpected relationships, trends, or fluctuations that may indicate areas of increased risk of material misstatement. These procedures focus the auditor''s attention on areas requiring additional audit procedures.
Wrong (A): Planning analytics are used for risk identification, not to provide substantive evidence about account balances. Substantive analytics serve that purpose.
Wrong (C): Planning analytics compare preliminary data to benchmarks for risk assessment, not to confirm prior-year statement accuracy.
Wrong (D): Analytical procedures during planning assess risk; testing operating effectiveness of controls is a separate audit procedure under AU-C 330.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4587;

-- Q4590: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s explanation for the difference without further investigation","Investigate the difference through inquiry and additional audit procedures","Immediately adjust the financial statements to match the auditor''s expectation","Ignore the difference if the recorded amount falls below overall materiality"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, when a substantive analytical procedure identifies a significant unexpected difference, the auditor must investigate by inquiring of management and obtaining corroborating evidence. The auditor assesses whether the difference results from a misstatement or a legitimate business factor.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s explanation without corroboration fails to meet the auditor''s obligation to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence under AU-C 520.
Wrong (C): The auditor does not adjust the financial statements; management is responsible for recording adjustments, and the auditor evaluates whether adjustments are needed.
Wrong (D): A significant unexpected difference warrants investigation regardless of whether the recorded amount is below materiality, as it may signal unidentified risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4590;

-- Q4591: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Replace substantive testing for accounts deemed to be immaterial","Help form an overall conclusion on financial statement consistency","Detect all remaining misstatements still present in the financial statements","Test the operating effectiveness of the entity''s internal controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 520, the final analytical review helps the auditor form an overall conclusion about whether the financial statements as a whole are consistent with the auditor''s understanding of the entity and its environment. It serves as a reasonableness check that may reveal previously unrecognized risks.
Wrong (A): The final analytical review is not designed to replace substantive testing; it is a required overall assessment performed after substantive work is complete.
Wrong (C): The overall review is a broad reasonableness check, not a procedure designed to detect all remaining misstatements in the financial statements.
Wrong (D): The overall review assesses financial statement consistency, not the operating effectiveness of internal controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4591;

